{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>In Jacob's tent and in Leah's tent.</b> Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 31:33): \"In Jacob's tent\" — that is Rachel's tent, for he was constantly with her. And so it says, \"the sons of Rachel, Jacob's wife\" (Genesis 46:19), and in the case of all the others it does not say \"Jacob's wife.\" \"And he came into Rachel's tent\" — when he went out from Leah's tent he returned to Rachel's tent before searching in the tent of the maidservants, and all this for what reason? Because he recognized that she was the one most suspected. But according to the plain sense, it is not correct that it should call it by two names in a single verse. And Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 31:33) said that he came into Jacob's tent and into Leah's tent and into the tent of the two maidservants, for there was one tent for both of them, and afterward he returned a second time to Leah's tent and after that to Rachel's tent. This too is not correct. He further wrote: \"And it seems likely to me that the verse took a shortcut and delayed to mention Rachel's tent, in order to state concerning all of them 'and he did not find,' for they were not there, and then it went back and explained that at the time of his going out from Leah's tent he came into Rachel's tent, where the teraphim were.\" This is correct. But the reason there were tents for all of them is that this was part of the modesty of the righteous man, for each of the four women had a designated tent, so that the other would not know when he came to her companion. It is also forbidden by Torah law, as the Sages mentioned in tractate Niddah (Niddah 17a), and Jacob had a designated tent, and there they would eat at his table with his sons and the members of his household. And the reason for \"Jacob's wife\" mentioned in connection with Rachel is, in my opinion, according to its plain sense, because she is mentioned among the concubines in that passage (Genesis 46:19), and therefore he did not say thus in the section \"And Jacob sent\" (Genesis 35:24–25), where he counts Leah and Rachel and afterward the maidservants."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra's third opinion is quoted in the second person as Ramban paraphrases it; the transition to 'And it seems likely to me' (והקרוב אלי) is Ibn Ezra's own words, not Ramban's, and has been rendered accordingly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:37:55.257916829Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}